@Jess
On a musical score, a SLUR is "a curved line spanning any number of notes of different pitches and indicates legato articulation".

Sorry for the half-written comment above. It apparently submitted itself while I was looking up the definition.

@Laura Stratton
I think a 'great solver' is simply one who doesn't give up, no matter the count of needed 'easy mode' clues, wiki look ups, and out and out cheats!  

And, of course, time needed.  Let the young, with their nimble, non-arthritic fingers boast of their scant times.  Meanwhile, I persevere!

@Peter Koetters

It’s apparently a maze where the goal is to find theee X’s, or strikes. But X’s for strikes is bowling, and in bowling, there’s no ump. 

I think it’s a mess.

@Matt

Re. the quotes on “hang”: I don’t think they were strictly *necessary*, but IMO that choice made sense.

“Veg” is awkward to the extreme in writing, whereas it’s very common as a spoken expression. I’ll stop short of claiming it’s “not a thing” as a written term…but I’d bet it almost always appears in quotes (i.e. in dialogue).

@Petrol

I know Blancmange — both the food and the band. Pretty far from a stew,at least in the modern sense.

Heretofore I thought of POTTAGE as a kind of porridge...

@Lpr

I did not know that, but now that you mention it it doesn't really surprise me. 

I love the idea that she might be reading this.

@SP
