@Jennifer
Space Invaders was released in 1978, which makes it 48 years old.

Come on, dude. Video games have been around for half a freakin century. You have no excuse for not knowing about them.

@retired, with cat

Is it important to you to feel like the constructor has a personal connection to the contents of a puzzle? If so, you must have been over the moon on Wednesday!

To me, this is a particular sub-genre of themeless (characterized by lots of ambiguous/underspecified clues, along with a fair bit of stretchy-ness), and while it happens to be my personal favorite kind of puzzle, I can definitely see what you’re talking about — there is a relatively bloodless sorta vibe to such puzzles.

@Dan
since you didn't ask:
It refers to a music track with the usual 4-beat thump that might delight dancers in a club.

@Dan
The last time I rocked out, the year started with 19– but I figured this out. It is a request for a raucous, high-intensity, no-limit piece of music suitable for a night out clubbing, on the topic of crosswords? 
I suspect the DJ couldn't find one.

@Bill
I did not know a slur could do that. Taking a moment of silence in respect.

Favorite chord was on the next page--7 accidentals. Layered three deep to fit them all in.

@Bill
Interesting. I recently read (was it here?) that there is a definite order for adjectives preceding a noun,  based on categories like size, colour, texture, etc. So, e.g., it's a small brown mouse, never a brown small mouse. 
I now realize I've observed those rules for my entire anglophone life without being consciously aware of them.

@SP

I must have missed that dust up.  

I very much agree.

@Peter
Anyone else try PLUPERFECT for 1A. No? It has the same number of letters as JENGA TOWER.
AMHIK.

@FJ

Again, I never comment because someone says “this was too hard for me” or “I thought these clues were too obscure”. But I do get defensive when people use “this was a terrible puzzle” as a synonym for “I couldn’t solve it” or even “I really thought this was too hard”. Except for HECKA I didn’t think anything was a particular stretch today (and that still within bounds of reason), although there were plenty of misdirections and some less commonly used words and slang. But again this is a PUZZLE and a weekend themeless. For puzzles you have to KNOW stuff and not everyone knows the same thing. But even so there is plenty of opportunity to figure it out in crosses or common word and letter patterns.

If you read the other comments lots of people thought these were reasonable clues and was a good puzzle to publish. You have to be pretty self-centered to assume that it’s a terrible puzzle and shouldn’t have been published based on your own knowledge base. Sorry, but I’m in that kind of mood.

@FJ

I love it when we bend a bit to meet each other halfway.

